            <ArticleTitle>Serous degeneration of bone marrow mimics spinal tumor.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>80-84</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <ELocationID EIdType="doi" ValidYN="Y">10.1007/s00586-016-4778-8</ELocationID>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To present a rare case of serous degeneration of bone marrow which resembles primary spinal tumor or bony metastasis to spine. Serous degeneration of bone marrow or gelatinous marrow transformation is a rare disease characterized by focal marrow hypoplasia, fat atrophy, and accumulation of extracellular mucopolysaccharides abundant in hyaluronic acid. Few literature was reviewed and few clinical case was presented.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Two cases of serous marrow transformation were reported.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">In the first case, a 29-year-old man suffered from severe left buttock pain. Bone metastasis was impressed in radiology examinations. Percutaneous endoscopic lumbar discectomy was performed along with bone biopsy. In the second case, a 49-year-old man presented lower back pain with radiation to bilateral lower legs. Magnetic resonance imaging revealed a water-like signal lesion in sacrum. Serous marrow transformation was confirmed pathologically in both cases.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">To the best of our knowledge, a case of serous degeneration of bone marrow resembling malignancy has not been reported in the literature. In this report, two cases demonstrate serous transformation of bone marrow mimics spinal tumor.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
